			<div class="content" id="post-content-2822031">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Typographical&nbsp;stuff</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I can't tell if some of this is intentional, as it's a log but seems unclinically written at points, so I'll just address points I saw.</p>
<p>&quot;The entity's Jaw opens&quot; - Jaw should not be capitalized.</p>
<p>&quot;reach their positions, and&quot; (occurs twice) + &quot;second vocalization, and&quot;, &quot;snakes from below SCP-3700-1, and&quot; - commas should not be used before &quot;and&quot; unless both clauses are independent. This occurs several more times throughout the log.</p>
<p>&quot;An audible crack is heard, as the Selkie is released, and the SCP-3456 entity wails&quot;.<br />
&quot;seen lifting several crew members, from the Selkie, who were displaced into the ocean, and places them safely&quot;<br />
These are additional commas that may be grammatically incorrect but seem to represent prosaic pauses, so it's unclear if they're correct.</p>
<p>&quot;All vessels, ANGIE&quot;, &quot;CENTAUR in the water!&quot;, &quot;Selkie, switch targets&quot; - these are the only three lines that represent dialogue from one of the ships that are not enclosed by quotation marks. I would edit these to remain consistent.</p>
<p>&quot;Its too close!&quot; - its should be it's.</p>
<p>&quot;We Read you Delta-7.&quot; - Read should not be capitalized.</p>
<p>&quot;Is there any trace of either entity.&quot; - Seems like it should end in a question mark to appropriately reflect intent.</p>
<p>&quot;Also Negative&quot; - Negative should not be capitalized.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>None of the errors I noted are prevalent outside of the incident log, but the log does strike me as hastily edited. That aside, it's an interesting take on a lesser-heralded branch of mythology with an interesting impact on the region and it's just plain fun to see monsters fighting in the ocean. Outside the errors noted in the log I quite like it, and if the log was tightened up it'd be an easy upvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
